 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server commits each edit as one event revision and pushes committed revisions to browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections. Browsers display a revisioned view of the board and, after a reconnect, catch up from their last applied revision.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Fast updates are obtained without a realtime vendor's recurring fees or provider coupling. The cost is owned operations: connection capacity for up to 800 browsers, reconnect handling, and replayable revision storage. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were considered and not chosen. Polling repeats reads from every browser and adds a few seconds of lag; a hosted service adds recurring fees and ties the board to one provider's subscription model.
+
+Change storage, delivery, and browser recovery are designed together under this approach. Revision history must support replay from a browser's last applied revision, and committed revisions must reach connected sessions reliably.
 
